  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 PM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nCurrent-Tonight... A complicated convective forecast exists through \nthe remainder of the afternoon and into the evening due to a complex \nlow pressure system sprawled across Florida. Looking at the large \npicture, broad mid-level troughing extends over the Florida \npeninsula. This energy aloft will set the stage for a conditional \nstrong storm environment through the remainder of the day with cold \ntemperatures around -10C analyzed aloft. Up to this point, \nconvection has struggled to become deep enough to take advantage of \nthe energy aloft. While cloudy conditions persisted over the \nsouthern portion of the forecast area early in the day, some \nclearing in vicinity of the I-4 region has allowed a local heating \nboundary to develop. This has helped to initialize scattered \nconvection near and north of the Orlando metro while development has \nstruggled to the south. However, will be watching for extra lift \nacross southern areas near Lake Okeechobee over the next few hours \nwhere RAP analysis indicates a low-level jet axis. Based on this \nanalysis and satellite/ radar trends, the current thinking is that\nareas across the north could perhaps see higher coverage of \nstorms, while areas across the south could show better dynamics \nfor a few stronger storms. Isolated strong or marginally severe \nstorms which become deep enough to take advantage energy aloft \nwill be capable of occasional to frequent lightning, gusty winds \n45-55 mph, and coin-sized hail. A brief tornado cannot be ruled \nout. Southwest steering flow 15-20 mph will maintain storm \nmovement, limiting prolonged rainfall accumulations over saturated\nareas. However, there is a 10% chance for localized rainfall \ntotals to 2 inches in moderate to heavy downpours across areas of \ncentral Florida.\n\nOvernight temperatures fall into the low 60s (north) to upper 60s \n(south). Watching for lowering stratus (or fog) late tonight into \nearly Monday, especially near, north, and west of I-4.\n\nSunday-Monday... A mid to upper level trough in vicinity of east \ncentral Florida early Sunday will lift northeast, with quasi-zontal \nflow building through the period. A surface front is pulled across \nthe area Sunday, as low pressure is guided northeastward along the \nGeorgia and Carolina coast. Offshore, westerly flow increases around \n10 mph Sunday afternoon, slackening and veering north into Monday. \nDrier air advecting across the region should keep rain chances below \n20% early in the week, however brief sprinkles cannot be ruled out \non Sunday. Despite drier conditions, flood waters from the recent \nheavy rainfall event will be slow to recede. These flooding impacts \nare expected to persist, especially near the St. Johns river and \nacross portions of Volusia/Brevard counties. Avoid flood roadways! \n\nTuesday-Friday... A mid-level low begins to dig through the midwest \nU.S. Tuesday. Troughing rounds the base of the low, with associated \nvorticity extending over the Florida peninsula into mid week. Mean \nglobal ensembles suggest an increase in moisture as an area of low \npressure attempts to develop slightly south or east of the local \narea. Mostly cloudy conditions are forecast Tuesday with a return of \nscattered rain chances by Wednesday. Drier air then builds Thursday \nbehind the next boundary passage. The warmest temperatures are \nforecast Tuesday with highs in the mid 70s (north) to near 80 \n(south). A slow cooling trend is then forecast each day, with \ntemperatures mostly spreading the low to mid 70s by Friday. Morning \nlows mostly range the 60s Tuesday and Wednesday mornings, falling \ninto the 50s near and north of I-4 Thursday and Friday.",
